  "grammarNotes": [
    {
      "id": "termine-days-months",
      "title": "Days and months",
      "body": "All days and months are **der** words: *der Montag, der Mai*.\n\nMost days end in **-tag** (day), said *tahk*: a *g* at the end of a word sounds like *k*. **Mittwoch** means *midweek*.\n\nThe months look a lot like English: *Januar, Februar, März, April, Mai, Juni, Juli, August, September, Oktober, November, Dezember*.",
      "table": [
        ["German", "English"],
        ["der Montag", "Monday"],
        ["der Dienstag", "Tuesday"],
        ["der Mittwoch", "Wednesday"],
        ["der Donnerstag", "Thursday"],
        ["der Freitag", "Friday"],
        ["der Samstag", "Saturday"],
        ["der Sonntag", "Sunday"]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "termine-am-im-um",
      "title": "am, im, um: saying when",
      "body": "German uses three little words to say *when*:\n\n- **um** + a clock time: *um zehn Uhr* (at ten o'clock)\n- **am** + a day: *am Montag* (on Monday), and *am Wochenende* (at the weekend)\n- **im** + a month: *im Mai* (in May)\n\n**heute, morgen** and **übermorgen** need no little word: *Morgen habe ich Zeit.*\n\nPut the day before the time: *am Montag um zehn Uhr*.",
      "table": [
        ["Word", "Used with", "Example"],
        ["um", "clock times", "um zehn Uhr"],
        ["am", "days, the weekend", "am Montag, am Wochenende"],
        ["im", "months", "im Juli"],
        ["(none)", "heute, morgen, übermorgen", "Morgen arbeite ich."]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "termine-modals",
      "title": "Modal verbs: können, müssen, wollen",
      "body": "**können** (can), **müssen** (must, to have to) and **wollen** (to want) are *modal verbs*. They follow their own pattern:\n\n- The singular has a different vowel: *ich kann*, *ich muss*, *ich will*.\n- **ich** and **er / sie / es** have no ending and look the same: *ich kann, sie kann*.\n- The plural is regular again.\n\nCareful: **ich will** means *I want*, not *I will*. To ask or order politely, use *möchten* instead.",
      "table": [
        ["Person", "können", "müssen", "wollen"],
        ["ich", "kann", "muss", "will"],
        ["du", "kannst", "musst", "willst"],
        ["er / sie / es", "kann", "muss", "will"],
        ["wir", "können", "müssen", "wollen"],
        ["ihr", "könnt", "müsst", "wollt"],
        ["sie / Sie", "können", "müssen", "wollen"]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "termine-word-order",
      "title": "Word order: verb second, infinitive last",
      "body": "The verb with the ending always comes **second** in a statement. With a modal verb, the second verb goes to the **very end**, in its infinitive form.\n\nYou can start with a time instead of the subject. The verb stays second, so the subject moves **after** it: *Morgen muss ich arbeiten.*\n\nIn a yes/no question the modal comes first: *Kannst du am Montag kommen?*\n\nWhen the meaning is clear, the second verb can be left out: *Am Montag kann ich nicht.* (I can't make it on Monday.)",
      "table": [
        ["1", "2 (verb)", "middle", "end"],
        ["Ich", "muss", "morgen", "arbeiten."],
        ["Morgen", "muss", "ich", "arbeiten."],
        ["Wir", "wollen", "am Sonntag", "kochen."],
        ["Am Montag", "kann", "ich leider nicht.", ""]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "termine-phrases",
      "title": "Making an appointment",
      "body": "Learn these as fixed phrases. Use the *Sie* forms with a doctor's practice or anyone you don't know well.\n\n**leider** (unfortunately) makes a *no* polite. **Das geht.** means *that's possible*, and **Das passt.** means *that suits me*.",
      "table": [
        ["You want to…", "Say"],
        ["ask if someone is free", "Haben Sie am Montag Zeit? / Hast du am Montag Zeit?"],
        ["ask when", "Wann haben Sie Zeit? / Wann hast du Zeit?"],
        ["suggest a time", "Geht es um zehn Uhr?"],
        ["say yes", "Das geht. / Das passt."],
        ["say no politely", "Am Dienstag kann ich leider nicht."],
        ["say it's fine", "Kein Problem!"],
        ["say goodbye", "Bis Montag!"]
      ]
    }
  ],
